{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>And make atonement for him, for that he sinned by reason of the soul.</b> [Meaning] that he did not guard himself from corpse-impurity. And Rabbi Elazar Hakappar says: because he afflicted himself [by abstaining] from wine (Nazir 3a). And this is wondrous, for one may wonder at his words: if he was commanded to bring two turtle-doves because he afflicted himself from wine, why does he bring this atonement specifically after he became impure through a corpse? And furthermore, the verse calls him 'holy' — so how can Rabbi Elazar say that he is called a sinner? And it appears that even Rabbi Elazar concedes that all the days during which his naziriteship continues he is called holy and not a sinner, for 'he is a nazir of Elohim.' However, if he became impure through a corpse, whose law is 'and the first days shall fall, because his naziriteship is defiled' — from this point forward we reckon, and retroactively the matter becomes clear: the first days were not included in the naziriteship at all. It is as though he afflicted himself with wine during those days for no reason, not during the time of his naziriteship. Therefore he is called a sinner on account of those first days, during which he is nothing but one who afflicts himself for nothing, without naziriteship and without holiness. And this is a precious interpretation." "Yet I have reason to explain Rabbi Elazar's words in another way, and to say that the nazirite is called a sinner because were he a blameless and upright man who conducted his affairs properly, he would not have needed to vow and to become a nazirite — for who would prevent him from conducting himself with asceticism and abstaining from luxuries without a vow? And from the fact that he needed to bind himself with naziriteship, this indicates that he knows himself that there is no restraint upon his spirit; therefore he closes up and swears against the evil impulse, and in this he brought affliction upon himself, for anything a man vows — his impulse presses against it all the more. And for this reason everyone who vows is called a sinner, as they learned (Nedarim 77b) from the verse ״וְכִי תֶחְדַּל לִנְדֹּר לֹא יִהְיֶה בְךָ חֵטְא״ (Deuteronomy 23:23) — because he provokes the evil impulse against himself. And they say to him: is it not enough for you what the Torah forbade upon you in the matter of the restraint of the evil impulse? Come and do not add to it. For in everything the Torah forbade — even though it caused us the provocation of the evil impulse, as it is written that it provokes itself against Israel more than against the nations (Sukkah 52b) — nevertheless, the two stand against it: the Written Torah and the Oral Torah." "But as for one who transgresses the prohibition of bal tosif [you shall not add] — lo, he adds further provocation of the evil impulse. Perhaps in this addition the Torah does not lend its strength to stand against it, for its strength extends only to a matter for which the Torah is the cause — 'for the one who struck, He shall heal.' So too this nazirite is called a sinner from two sides: both from the side that before this naziriteship he was like a breached city with no restraint upon his spirit, until he needed to bind himself with naziriteship; and from the side that while a nazirite he provokes the evil impulse against himself. Nevertheless, if he stands against it, he is called 'holy' — for all the evil impulse's designs are to breach his fence. And this is what it says: ״וְכִי יָמוּת עָלָיו מֵת בְּפֶתַע פִּתְאוֹם״ and so forth — this indicates that all this is the deed of the Satan, who brought to his hand this occurrence of impurity suddenly, by compulsion and without his knowledge, in order to defile the head of his naziriteship. Therefore he needed atonement 'for that he sinned by reason of the soul.' 'And he afflicted himself from wine' — for through this affliction he caused the evil impulse to provoke itself against him, and it contrived circumstances to defile his naziriteship. And furthermore he needed atonement because he afflicted himself, for instead of serving HaShem with joy, he served with affliction. And joy is an indication of the perfection of the one who acts, as it is written (Psalms 139:24): ״וּרְאֵה אִם דֶּרֶךְ עֹצֶב בִּ״ and so forth — for had he rejoiced in this naziriteship, he would have guarded himself with extra care from impurity; but since he did not guard himself, this indicates that he was not rejoicing in this naziriteship, and in this the evil impulse found a place to foil from him all that he had schemed to do. ״וְצַדִּיקִים יִשְׂמְחוּ וְיַעַלְזוּ לִפְנֵי ה׳ וְיָשִׂישׂוּ בְשִׂמְחָה״ (ibid. 68:4) — the בֵּית of בְשִׂמְחָה means 'in the merit of' the joy with which they served HaShem, as stated."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {2 "The phrase 'כִּי הַמּוֹחֵץ הוּא יִרְפָּא' is rendered literally; it appears to be an Aramaic medical maxim meaning 'the one who struck, He shall heal,' applied as a principle that the Torah's remedial power applies only where the Torah itself introduced the restriction. The citation of Psalms 139:24 uses only its opening words followed by 'וג׳' as in the source; the verse continues 'וּנְחֵנִי בְּדֶרֶךְ עוֹלָם.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:40:17.213403795Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}